---
/**
 * Where a submission lands.
 *
 * Somebody has just spent ten minutes writing carefully, pressed a button, and watched
 * everything they wrote disappear from the screen. The job of this page is to make that
 * feel like an arrival rather than a loss: say what happened, say what happens next, say
 * when, and say what to do if they want to change something.
 *
 * It is deliberately specific about the wait. "Pending review" with no timescale reads as
 * "possibly never", and a volunteer queue that takes a few days is fine as long as nobody
 * has to guess.
 */
import Account from '../../layouts/Account.astro';
import { path } from '../../lib/paths';
import { LICENCE, SITE } from '../../lib/site';
---

<Account
  title="Submitted"
  description="What happens to a report after it is submitted."
  lead="It is in the queue. Nothing else is needed from you."
  gate="required"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What happens next</h2>

    <ul class="tombstone-list">
      <li>
        A volunteer moderator reads it. Moderation here is careful and slow rather than
        instant — expect <strong>a few days</strong>, not minutes, and longer over a
        holiday.
      </li>
      <li>
        When it is published it appears in the listings, sorted by recency, with an open
        tombstone: nobody has confirmed yet that it still works.
      </li>
      <li>
        Nothing about it is public until then. It is visible to you and to moderators, and
        to nobody else.
      </li>
      <li>
        Published accounts are licensed <a href={LICENCE.url} rel="license">{LICENCE.name}</a>.
        You keep copyright and are credited under whatever name your profile shows.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If you want to change it</h2>

    <p>
      A pending submission can still be edited — the database allows its author to change it
      right up until a moderator publishes it. The screen for doing that is not built yet,
      so for the moment the way to amend or withdraw one is to write to <a
        href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a
      >. Say which submission and what you want changed.
    </p>

    <p>
      After it is published the text is fixed, which is deliberate: people confirm that a
      report still works, and those confirmations would otherwise attest to a version
      nobody can read any more. Deleting a published report is always possible.
    </p>

    <h2>The most useful thing you could do now</h2>

    <p>
      Post a second one about something that <em>did not</em> work. Accounts of failure are
      the ones nobody publishes and the ones this corpus is short of — and they are usually
      quicker to write, because you already know where it went wrong.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="button button--primary" href={path('/reports/new/')}>Post another</a>
    <a class="button" href={path('/account/')}>Back to your profile</a>
  </div>
</Account>
